/-!
# Loci in the rational Taylor chart

Let `Q` be a differential polynomial of order `r` over a field `F`, fix a center, and write `S`
for the initial separant. This file describes, as zero loci in the initial jet space `F^{r+1}`,
the sets that the rational Taylor chart of `Q` cuts out.

* `highTaylorCutsIdeal center Q K k τ` is the ideal generated by the common numerators
  `commonTaylorNumerator center Q τ l` for `k ≤ l < K`. On `S ≠ 0` its zeros are the initial jets
  whose reconstructed polynomial has degree below `k`.
* `regularAgreementCutLocus I center Q K τ domain received` is the set of zeros of an ideal `I` at
  which `S ≠ 0` and every agreement equation `taylorAgreementEquation` at
  `(domain i, received i)` vanishes. When `I` contains the high cuts and the agreement points
  include `k` distinct ones, this set has at most one point.
* `initialJetPrimeFamily center Q` is the finite family of minimal primes over the initial
  equation that do not contain `S`. Every zero of the initial equation with `S ≠ 0`, over any
  extension field, lies on one of them. Each member has dimension `r` (its affine Hilbert
  polynomial has natural degree `r`), and for every weight `b` the potential
  `∑ P, affineDegree P * b ^ dim P` of the family is at most `jetTotalDegree Q * b ^ r`.

The last part places polynomial solutions in these loci. In an infinite domain, finitely many
solutions with nonzero separant specialization share a center at which all their separants are
nonzero. At such a center, the Hasse jet of a solution of degree below `k` lies on the initial
hypersurface and on the high cuts, and the jets of distinct solutions of degree below `K` are
distinct.

## Main statements

* `mem_zeroLocus_highTaylorCutsIdeal_iff`: the zeros of the high cuts.
* `regularAgreementCutLocus_subsingleton` and `eq_of_mem_zeroLocus_of_highTaylorCutsIdeal_le`:
  high cuts and agreement at `k` distinct points leave at most one regular jet.
* `exists_mem_initialJetPrimeFamily_of_regular`: regular points of the initial hypersurface lie
  on retained prime components.
* `natDegree_affineHilbertPolynomial_of_mem_initialJetPrimeFamily` and
  `sum_affineDegree_mul_pow_initialJetPrimeFamily_le_jetTotalDegree`: the dimension and the
  potential of the retained components.
* `exists_forall_jetEvaluation_ne_zero_of_family`: a common center for a finite family of
  differential equations and solutions.
* `exists_forall_jetEvaluation_ne_zero`: a common center for finitely many solutions of one
  differential equation.
* `injOn_polynomialJet` and `card_image_polynomialJet`: Hasse jets separate regular solutions.
* `polynomialJet_mem_zeroLocus_initialJetEquation_sup_highTaylorCutsIdeal` and
  `polynomialJet_mem_regularAgreementCutLocus`: regular solutions lie in these loci.

## References

* [Dao, Q., Kominers, S. D., and Thaler, J., *Reed–Solomon Codes Beyond Johnson: Efficient
  Decoding and Smaller Cryptographic Proofs*][DKT26], Appendix A.6.
-/

namespace PolynomialDifferential

